---
/**
 * Problem spoke — "grass burs (sandburs) all over my yard."
 *
 * THE ARGUMENT: this is a soil-temperature problem and a seed-bank problem, not
 * a spraying problem. The trigger is 52°F soil temperature, the honest horizon
 * is years rather than a season, and the strongest long-term measure is dense
 * turf — which IS a sourced claim, not folk wisdom.
 *
 * VERIFIED:
 *  - The 52°F trigger, consistent across EVERY source: "generally when the soil
 *    temperature (NOT the air temperature) reaches 52 degrees F" (AgriLife
 *    Austin County); "Seed germination starts when soil temperatures reach 52 F
 *    and peaks at 75 F" (OSU); "One to three seeds in the sandbur pod will
 *    germinate once soil temperatures reach ~52°F" (AgriLife 2025 Grassburs).
 *  - Boeri (AgriLife statewide turfgrass specialist, AgriLife Today, 13 Aug
 *    2024): "Sandbur seeds can begin early germination at soil temperatures of
 *    52 degrees and continue to germinate at low levels through the summer.
 *    However, peak emergence is around April-May when soils reach 72 degrees.";
 *    "Seeds can survive up to eight years in the soil."; "Sandburs have
 *    extended germination periods that can last more than three years."; "The
 *    best long-term strategy to win the war against sandburs is to create
 *    dense, healthy turfgrass."
 *  - Univ. of Arizona: "It will take at least three years of control effort
 *    (probably longer)."; "Each bur usually contains 2 seeds. There may be as
 *    many as 1,000 seeds produced by a single plant."; flowers May to October.
 *  - AgriLife Controlling Field Sandbur in Turfgrass: "is especially adapted to
 *    dry, sandy soils"; "Field sandburs (grassburs) are generally not a problem
 *    in well maintained turfgrass areas."; "A dense stand of healthy grass
 *    provides the best weed control."; apply pre-emergent "two to four weeks
 *    ahead of germination"; heavy infestations "EVERY 6 WEEKS through
 *    September"; light infestations "two applications that are 6 weeks
 *    apart"; higher cut → lower infestation; deep-soak watering maintaining "a
 *    dry surface layer" favours the grass; weeds are "opportunists" that
 *    "invade weakened lawns".
 *  - OSU: the bur "absorbs and retains soil moisture until conditions are good
 *    for germination. In this way, sandbur can easily take over poorly managed
 *    bermudagrass pastures on dry sandy soils."; "keeps the seeds viable for
 *    years in the soil"; "Proper fertilization and stocking rate combined are
 *    the best sandbur control."; Plateau and Pastora "controlled sandbur
 *    best…when applied within three weeks after germination."
 *  - AgriLife Ag News and Views (Jan 2025): three native Texas species — field,
 *    longspine and southern sandbur; field sandbur most problematic because it
 *    "over-winters or perennializes"; two germination periods, spring and fall.
 *  - AgriLife 2025 Grassburs: "February is the time for forage and turf
 *    managers to begin waging war against this pest"; "Working from South to
 *    North Texas, the beginning to the end of February is an ideal month".
 *
 * ⚠️ FIVE HONESTY FLAGS THAT MUST SURVIVE ANY EDIT:
 *  1. DO NOT STATE FLATLY THAT SANDBUR IS AN ANNUAL. AgriLife sources
 *     contradict each other: the homeowner PDF names C. echinatus and calls it
 *     a summer annual; AggieTurf names C. spinifex and lists "Annual or
 *     Perennial"; Ag News says field sandbur "over-winters or perennializes";
 *     OSU says annual that "rarely, behaves as a short-lived perennial." Say
 *     warm-season and primarily annual, and report the disagreement.
 *  2. UPPER GERMINATION TEMPERATURE CONFLICTS. "Germination ceases at about
 *     74°F" (AgriLife) vs peak at 72°F with continued low-level summer
 *     germination (Boeri) vs peaks at 75°F with emergence into late fall (OSU).
 *     The 52°F START is consistent; the ceiling is not. Do not assert that
 *     germination stops.
 *  3. NO WEST TEXAS CALENDAR DATE EXISTS. AgriLife publishes dates for south,
 *     central and north Texas only — roughly 1 March, 15 March and 1 April.
 *     There is NO South Plains or Permian Basin date in any source. Any local
 *     timing on this page must be presented as inferred from the 52°F rule and
 *     the reader pointed at soil temperature, which the sources stress is SOIL,
 *     not air.
 *  4. NAME NO HERBICIDE ACTIVES. The older AgriLife documents name MSMA/DSMA
 *     and imazaquin; MSMA's residential turf registration has been restricted
 *     by EPA and those documents are not recent. Newer AgriLife material
 *     centres on indaziflam and Celsius, which are professional products. No
 *     source gives a homeowner-current list. This page therefore names NO
 *     active ingredients and sends the reader to the current label. That is a
 *     deliberate choice, not an omission.
 *  5. THE EIGHT-YEAR FIGURE IS ONE PERSON IN A NEWS RELEASE. It is attributed
 *     on the page to AgriLife's statewide turfgrass specialist by role rather
 *     than presented as a study finding. The peer-reviewed seed-germination
 *     paper was unreachable (403).
 *  Also: "sandbur thrives because of low fertility" is only PARTLY supported.
 *  The sourced framing is that it exploits thin, poorly managed turf on dry
 *  sandy soils — which is what the page says.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../../../components/Cta.astro';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Services', path: '/services/' },
  { name: 'Lawn care', path: '/services/lawn-care/' },
  { name: 'Grass burs', path: '/services/lawn-care/grassburs/' },
];

const quickAnswer =
